    <div>
      <h2>Anxiety as a nervous system state</h2>
      <p>Anxiety is easier to understand as a threat response that never got to finish than as
      a thinking error. That is why insight so often fails to help: you can know the fear is
      disproportionate and still have a chest that will not unclench.</p>
      <p>Somatic Experiencing works in doses, tracking sensation and the small movements the
      body did not complete, which widens your window of tolerance rather than talking you out
      of the feeling. NARM asks where the pattern was learned and what it was protecting. When
      specific memories or triggers are reliably hijacking you, EMDR gets added.</p>
      <p>Where anxiety sits on top of developmental trauma, which is common, the work looks
      like <a href="/therapy/trauma/">the trauma page</a> rather than symptom management.</p>
      <h3>What people bring here</h3>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>A body braced at rest, with nothing in particular happening.</li>
        <li>Panic attacks, and then anxiety about the next panic attack.</li>
        <li>Social anxiety: rehearsing conversations before and reviewing them after.</li>
        <li>Anxiety that lives in the chest and stomach more than in the thoughts.</li>
        <li>Sleep that will not arrive, or arrives and does not restore.</li>
        <li>Coping that works well enough at work and collapses at home.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Social anxiety specifically</h3>
      <p>Social anxiety is usually relational in origin, so it responds to relational work.
      NARM's question is not how to perform better in the room but what you learned about
      being seen, and what it cost to be yourself in front of other people.</p>
      <p>The pacing matters more than usual here. Being asked to push into exposure before
      your system has any footing tends to confirm the fear rather than disprove it.</p>
