<section class="shell band faq">
  <h2>Questions about anxiety therapy</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>Do you treat panic attacks?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Yes. Panic is usually treated as a nervous system event rather than a thought to
      correct: what the system was bracing for, what the response never completed, and how to
      build tolerance in doses so the next wave is survivable rather than catastrophic.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Is social anxiety treated differently from general anxiety?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>The lens shifts. Social anxiety is usually relational in origin, so NARM's questions
      about being seen and what it cost to be yourself in front of others do more work than
      symptom management. Both are covered here, and the consult is where we work out which lens
      fits what you are dealing with.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do you do exposure work?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Not as the main method. The Somatic Experiencing approach is titrated, meaning small
      doses at a pace your system can integrate, because a system that gets flooded learns that
      attention to itself is dangerous. Capacity comes first, contact with the feared thing
      after.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do I need medication for anxiety?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>That is a question for a prescriber. Reem is a Licensed Professional Counselor
      Supervisor, not a physician, and does not prescribe or advise on medication. Therapy and
      medication are not alternatives to each other, and plenty of people do both.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Does anxiety therapy work online?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Yes, and it is the default here. Body-based work translates well to video because it
      tracks your sensation rather than the room. Being in your own space is an advantage for
      some anxious systems and a distraction for others, which is worth raising in the
      consult.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>How soon does anxiety ease?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Stabilization work often shifts something within a handful of sessions. Anxiety that
      sits on developmental trauma takes longer, because the target is capacity rather than a
      symptom. We review it together instead of promising a number at the start.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
